{"model": "Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B-Base", "track": "hard", "task": "hard-modular", "seed": 2, "score": 1.0, "checks": [{"type": "number", "score": 1.0}], "secs": 3.811, "eval_count": 407, "truncated": false, "cut_in_thinking": false, "answer": "To find the remainder when \\(7^{2026}\\) is divided by 5, we can use modular arithmetic. Here's the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Simplify the base modulo 5:**\n   \\[\n   7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(7^{2026} \\equiv 2^{2026} \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n2. **Find the pattern of powers of 2 modulo 5:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   2^1 &\\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\\\\n   2^2 &\\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\\\\n   2^3 &\\equiv 8 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\\\\n   2^4 &\\equiv 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\\\\n   2^5 &\\equiv 32 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\\\\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   The powers of 2 modulo 5 repeat every 4 exponents: 2, 4, 3, 1.\n\n3. **Determine the position in the cycle:**\n   Since the cycle length is 4, we can find the remainder when 2026 is divided by 4:\n   \\[\n   2026 \\div 4 = 506 \\text{ with a remainder of } 2\n   \\]\n   So, \\(2^{2026} \\equiv 2^2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   2^2 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(7^{2026}\\) is divided by 5 is:\n\n**Answer: 4**"}
